<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="yes"?><p:sld xmlns:a="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/drawingml/2006/main" xmlns:r="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/officeDocument/2006/relationships" xmlns:mc="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/markup-compatibility/2006" xmlns:mv="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:mac:vml" xmlns:p="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/presentationml/2006/main" xmlns:c="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/drawingml/2006/chart" xmlns:dgm="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/drawingml/2006/diagram" xmlns:o="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:office" xmlns:v="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:vml" xmlns:pvml="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:powerpoint" xmlns:com="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/drawingml/2006/compatibility" xmlns:p14="http://schemas.microsoft.com/office/powerpoint/2010/main" xmlns:p15="http://schemas.microsoft.com/office/powerpoint/2012/main" ><p:cSld><p:spTree><p:nvGrpSpPr><p:cNvPr id="586" name="Shape 586"/><p:cNvGrpSpPr/><p:nvPr/></p:nvGrpSpPr><p:grpSpPr><a:xfrm><a:off x="0" y="0"/><a:ext cx="0" cy="0"/><a:chOff x="0" y="0"/><a:chExt cx="0" cy="0"/></a:xfrm></p:grpSpPr><p:pic><p:nvPicPr><p:cNvPr descr="Molecular structures of tetracycline and chloramphenicol.
Tetracycline has four hexagons bonded together. The first has alternating double-bonded sides. The bottom point bonds down to OH. The second hexagon bonds up and left to H3C and up and right to OH, and the bottom point double bonds down to O. The third hexagon has a double-sided bottom left side, and the bottom point bonds down to OH. Where the third and fourth hexagons meet, H (at the top) bonds down to OH (at the bottom). The last hexagon has a double-bonded right side, and the top point bonds up to N, which bonds up and left to H3C and up and right to CH3. The top right point bonds up and right to OH. The bottom right point bonds down and right to CONH2, and the bottom point double bonds down to O. In chloramphenicol, O2N bonds right to a benzene molecule, which bonds right to CH, which bonds down to OH and right to CH. This CH bonds up to NH, which bonds right to C, which double bonds down to O, and bonds right to CHCl2. CH also bonds down to CH2, which bonds down to OH. 

Molecular structures of cycloheximide, and streptomycin. 
Cycloheximide begins with a hexagon, where the left point bonds left to H3C, the top right point bonds up and right to CH3, the right point double bonds right to O, and the bottom right point bonds down and right to CHOH, which bonds down to CH2 which bonds down to a pentagon. The bottom left point double bonds down and left to O, the bottom point is N over H, and the bottom right point double bonds down and right to O. Streptomycin begins with an elongated hexagon with thick bottom sides. At the top left point H bonds down to CH2OH (inside). At the left point, HO bonds down to H. At the bottom left point, H (inside) bonds down to OH, and at the bottom right side, H3CN (H over N) (inside) bonds down to H. The right point bonds down and right to H and bonds up and right to O, which bonds down and right to the left point of a pentagon. This bonds down and left to H. At the bottom point OH (inside) bonds down to CHO. At the right point, CH3 bonds down to H, and the top right point is O. The top left point has H (inside) bonding up to O, which bonds up to H as the left point of an elongate hexagon. At the bottom left point, OH (inside) bonds down to H. At the bottom right point, H (inside) bonds down to OH. At the right point, H bonds up to NH, which bonds right to C, which double bonds up to NH and bonds right to NH2. At the top right point, H (inside) bonds down to HO (inside). At the top left point, H bonds up to NH, which bonds left to C, which double bonds up to NH and bonds left to H2N. 
